C - POINTERS
=============

Variables that store memory addresses.


WHAT IS A POINTER
-----------------
A pointer holds the memory address of another variable.

int x = 5;
int *ptr = &x;   ← ptr points to x


SYNTAX
------
int *ptr;           Declare pointer to int
*ptr = 10;          Change value at address
int value = *ptr;   Read value at address
&variable;          Get address of variable


OPERATORS
---------
&   Get address (address-of)
*   Get value (dereference)


EXAMPLE
-------
int age = 25;
int *ptr_age = &age;

printf("%d\n", *ptr_age);      Prints: 25
printf("%p\n", ptr_age);       Prints: 0x7fff5fbff8ac


WHY USE POINTERS
----------------
• Pass by reference
• Dynamic memory
• Complex data structures
• Efficient function calls


COMMON ERRORS
-------------
*ptr without declaring → Compiler error
NULL pointer access → Crash
Wrong type pointer → Wrong data


TIPS
----
Draw diagrams to understand.
Pointers take practice.
Start simple with single pointers.
